Hi Marlene — your boss may **not** be right. Being paid a salary does **not automatically** mean you can’t receive overtime.

Under U.S. federal law, many salaried workers must receive overtime—usually **1½ times their regular hourly rate for hours over 40 in a workweek**—unless they meet specific exemption rules.

For the common “executive, administrative, or professional” exemptions, you generally must:

- Be paid at least the required weekly salary threshold (currently **$684 per week**, or $35,568 per year, under federal rules);
- Be paid on a true salary basis; **and**
- Perform particular exempt duties.

At $38,000 per year, you meet the federal salary amount, but your **actual job duties** matter. An office manager is not automatically exempt. If you mainly do routine office work, follow detailed instructions, and do not have real authority over important business matters or employees, you may still be entitled to overtime. State law may give you stronger rights.

If you work 60 hours weekly, keep a simple record of:

- Each day’s start and stop times;
- Your unpaid breaks;
- Your duties and instructions;
- Pay stubs and messages about your hours;
- Any request that you work off the clock.

Do not alter records or secretly record conversations if that may violate your state’s law. You can calmly ask your employer, preferably in writing: **“Please explain which overtime exemption you believe applies to my position and how my duties satisfy it.”** Do not assume signing a policy or being called “salaried” gives up overtime rights.

You can contact your state labor department or the U.S. Department of Labor’s Wage and Hour Division at **1-866-487-9243**. A local legal-aid office or employment lawyer may also review this, often at no initial cost. Retaliating against you for asking about overtime or filing a complaint is generally illegal.

The exact answer depends heavily on **your state and what you actually do**. Also, overtime claims have deadlines, so it is wise to get advice soon.
